 resources, leading to slowdowns or complete unavailability. Managing the volume of 'requisições' (requests) is crucial to maintaining system stability and ensuring continuous service delivery. [Data: Entities (2190, 2215, 2224, 2227,+more); Relationships (2972, 3037, 3002,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Security and Control Considerations in Client-Server Networks\",\n            \"explanation\": \"While the client-server model offers enhanced security and control due to its centralized nature, this centralization also introduces specific security concerns. Protecting the central server becomes paramount, as it manages sensitive data and critical services. The need to secure this core component has made 'segurança como preocupação' (security as a concern) a significant aspect of deploying and managing client-server systems. [Data: Entities (2185, 2247,+more); Relationships (2966, 3072,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Infrastructure: Servers and Network Connectivity\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Servers are integral parts of a network infrastructure and rely on network devices like switches for communication. Servers connect to switches to facilitate communication within a local network, enabling the exchange of data and services between the server and its clients. This connectivity is essential for the client-server model to function effectively, ensuring that requests and responses can be transmitted reliably. [Data: Relationships (695,+more); Entities (637,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Cost Factor in Server Acquisition\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The acquisition of a server represents a significant financial investment, often referred to as the 'custo do servidor' (server cost). Implementing a client-server architecture involves upfront expenses for hardware and potentially software licenses. This cost is a key consideration for organizations planning to adopt or e